{"name":"Chrome Aluminium","hex":"#a8a9ad","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"chrome","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Chromium, when used to plate other metals.","The basic structural elements used in a graphical user interface, such as window frames and scroll bars, as opposed to the content."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To plate with chrome.","To treat with a solution of potassium bichromate, as in dyeing."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/chrome"},{"term":"aluminium","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A light, silvery metal extracted from bauxite, and a chemical element (symbol Al) with an atomic number of 13.\n\n An atom of this element.","An atom of this element."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/aluminium"}],"entries":[{"title":"Aluminium","blocks":[{"text":"Aluminium or aluminum is a chemical element; it has symbol Al and atomic number 13. It has a density lower than other common metals, about one-third that of steel. Aluminium has a great affinity toward oxygen, forming a protective layer of oxide on the surface when exposed to air. It visually resembles silver, both in its color and in its great ability to reflect light. It is soft, nonmagnetic, and ductile. It has one stable isotope, 27Al, which is highly abundant, making aluminium the 12th-most abundant element in the universe. The radioactivity of 26Al leads to it being used in radiometric dating."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5d/Aluminium-4.jpg/330px-Aluminium-4.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aluminium","wikidata":"Q663"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d7/Lingot_aluminium.jpg/500px-Lingot_aluminium.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d7/Lingot_aluminium.jpg/500px-Lingot_aluminium.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Aluminium ingot","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ALingot_aluminium.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Elemental_Bullion.jpg/500px-Elemental_Bullion.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Elemental_Bullion.jpg/1280px-Elemental_Bullion.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Elemental_Bullion.jpg/500px-Elemental_Bullion.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Three 1.0 pound (0.454 kg) bullion ingots of triple nine aluminium, iron, and copper etched with their scientific details. ","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AElemental_Bullion.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d9/Aluminium_Atomic_lattice.png/500px-Aluminium_Atomic_lattice.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d9/Aluminium_Atomic_lattice.png/500px-Aluminium_Atomic_lattice.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"High-resolution STEM-HAADF micrograph of Al atoms viewed along the [001] zone axis.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAluminium_Atomic_lattice.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/83/AlHydrolysis.png/250px-AlHydrolysis.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/83/AlHydrolysis.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/83/AlHydrolysis.png/250px-AlHydrolysis.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=p